The rotary embedding application in `WanAttnProcessor` builds two full-size
temporaries and then copies each into a strided half of the output.  Measured
(out/videogen_more_shape, region `attn_rope`, 0.4% irregular):

    attn_rope = 5,764.3*seq_len + 320,506        (inner_dim 64, i.e. 45 instructions
                                                  per rotary'd element)

against, in the same attention, `attn_qkv = 1,622.5*seq_len + 3,023.2*kv_len +
405,871` and `attn_sdpa = 157*seq_len + 1.9*kv_len + 97,709`.  Not one of the
top functions behind the 5,764 is arithmetic: they are
`c10::function_ref<...>::callback` (3,064), `at::internal::serial_for_each`
(1,264) and `at::DimCounter::increment` (352) -- TensorIterator's scalar
fallback, forced by the stride-2 interleaved layout, plus one allocation and
two whole extra passes for the temporaries.

Writing the products straight into the two halves of the output with `out=`
removes the temporaries and the two copies.  The expressions and their order
are unchanged, so the result is bit-for-bit what the original produced
(equiv_wan.py: latents and video identical at 5 grid points; equiv_more.py:
the processor's output identical at 6 (seq_len, heads, head_dim) points).
The closure is hoisted to module scope while we are here; nothing in it
captured the call, and it was rebuilt on every attention of every layer of
every forward.

--- a/src/diffusers/models/transformers/transformer_wan.py
+++ b/src/diffusers/models/transformers/transformer_wan.py
@@ -65,6 +65,38 @@
     return key_img, value_img
 
 
+def _apply_rotary_emb(
+    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,
+    freqs_cos: torch.Tensor,
+    freqs_sin: torch.Tensor,
+) -> torch.Tensor:
+    """Rotary embedding for Wan, applied to one of (query, key).
+
+    Wan interleaves the rotary pairs, so `x1`, `x2`, `cos` and `sin` all have
+    stride 2 in their last dimension and TensorIterator has to fall back to its
+    scalar DimCounter loop; the elementwise cost here is host-side iteration,
+    not arithmetic.  Writing the two results into `out[..., 0::2]` and
+    `out[..., 1::2]` builds a full-size contiguous temporary for each and then
+    copies it into the strided half -- two extra passes over the tensor and one
+    extra allocation per call, on every attention of every layer of every
+    forward.  Writing the same expressions straight into those halves with
+    `out=` removes them; the operations and their order are unchanged, so the
+    result is bit-for-bit what the original produced.
+
+    This used to be a closure inside `WanAttnProcessor.__call__`, rebuilt on
+    every attention call; nothing in it captures the call, so it lives here.
+    """
+    x = hidden_states.unflatten(-1, (-1, 2))
+    x1, x2 = x[..., 0], x[..., 1]
+    cos = freqs_cos[..., 0::2]
+    sin = freqs_sin[..., 1::2]
+    out = torch.empty_like(x)
+    torch.sub(x1 * cos, x2 * sin, out=out[..., 0])
+    torch.add(x1 * sin, x2 * cos, out=out[..., 1])
+    return out.flatten(-2).type_as(hidden_states)
+
+
 class WanAttnProcessor:
     _attention_backend = None
     _parallel_config = None
